1
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:05,892
Megalodon. The largest predatory
shark that has ever lived.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:12,023
But how big was it, really? Let's find
out, with everything drawn to true scale.

3
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:17,273
First, a ruler. A diver:

4
00:00:17,273 --> 00:00:25,233
about one point eight metres tall. Every animal
you'll see is drawn to the same scale as this person.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,115
A bull shark. The largest females
reach around three and a half metres.

6
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:34,765
A great white.

7
00:00:34,765 --> 00:00:41,283
Most adults are four to five metres long.
The biggest reliably measured, about six.

8
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:46,546
For a long time, this was the shape
scientists imagined for megalodon:

9
00:00:46,546 --> 00:00:48,759
a great white, scaled up.

10
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:52,695
A school bus. Twelve metres.

11
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:57,118
A whale shark, the largest fish alive today.

12
00:00:57,118 --> 00:01:05,960
Big adults reach about twelve metres. The largest reliable
record is close to nineteen. It feeds on plankton.

13
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:14,629
An orca. Large males reach about eight
metres. Today, it is the ocean's top predator.

14
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:22,179
A humpback whale. Around fifteen metres,
with flippers a third as long as its body.

15
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,363
And now... megalodon.

16
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:32,570
Here is the honest part.

17
00:01:32,570 --> 00:01:45,884
No complete skeleton has ever been found. A shark's skeleton is cartilage, and cartilage
rarely fossilises. What survives are teeth, and a few rare columns of vertebrae.

18
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,092
The teeth are enormous.

19
00:01:48,092 --> 00:01:54,339
The largest are around eighteen centimetres
long, about the length of a human hand.

20
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:59,999
For decades, size estimates scaled
a great white up from those teeth:

21
00:01:59,999 --> 00:02:03,321
fifteen metres, eighteen, perhaps twenty.

22
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:10,585
Then came a column of a hundred and forty-one
vertebrae from Belgium, all from one animal.

23
00:02:10,585 --> 00:02:14,746
Eleven metres of backbone,
without its head or tail.

24
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:24,014
Growth rings in those vertebrae, like the rings of a tree,
say this shark was about forty-six years old when it died.

25
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,859
Studies in twenty twenty-four and twenty twenty-five
argued that megalodon was not a giant great white at all.

26
00:02:32,859 --> 00:02:38,288
It was probably longer and slimmer,
closer in shape to a lemon shark.

27
00:02:39,250 --> 00:02:43,829
On that reading, the Belgian shark
was about sixteen metres long.

28
00:02:43,829 --> 00:02:48,204
That is our best-grounded
figure for a real individual.

29
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,092
Larger ones certainly existed.

30
00:02:51,092 --> 00:03:03,654
A single huge vertebra from Denmark suggests some may have reached twenty-four metres.
The researchers call that the upper limit the evidence can support. Most were smaller.

31
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:14,746
Megalodon lived from about twenty-three million to three point
six million years ago, in warm seas across much of the world.

32
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:20,097
Computer models suggest a bite
of more than ten tonnes of force.

33
00:03:20,097 --> 00:03:24,401
But those are models, built
by scaling up a great white.

34
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:33,980
Chemistry locked in its teeth suggests it was partly
warm-blooded, about seven degrees warmer than the water around it.

35
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:38,470
A powerful engine, that needed
enormous amounts of food.

36
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:49,760
Then, it vanished. The likely causes: a cooling climate and falling
seas that shrank the shallow nurseries where its young were born;

37
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:56,735
changes in the whales it hunted; and a newcomer
competing for the same prey. The great white.

38
00:03:58,250 --> 00:03:59,295
And it is gone.

39
00:03:59,295 --> 00:04:15,893
It is not hiding in the deep. Its pups grew up in shallow coastal water, and its teeth fill the fossil record, until
about three and a half million years ago. Then, nothing. The television specials that said otherwise were fiction.

40
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:23,002
But megalodon was not the
largest animal of all time.

41
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,938
That animal is alive today.

42
00:04:25,938 --> 00:04:32,109
The blue whale. Up to about thirty metres
long, and well over a hundred tonnes.

43
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:41,143
In twenty twenty-three, a fossil whale from Peru,
Perucetus, was proposed as possibly even heavier.

44
00:04:41,143 --> 00:04:47,703
Later work challenged that estimate. For
now, the blue whale still holds the record.

45
00:04:51,250 --> 00:04:54,819
The largest predator the
sea has ever known is gone.

46
00:04:55,750 --> 00:05:00,814
For almost twenty million years, it
passed over everything in the ocean.

47
00:05:01,750 --> 00:05:03,779
Then, one day, it didn't.

